    <node id="P031-C0001"><data key="label">We present a math-only framework in which theories arise as fixed points of idempotent operators and open-endedness requires changing the operator rather than iterating it. Under minimal hypotheses of composability plus limited interface access with bounded o…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0002"><data key="label">Interoperability of certificates (toolkit safety): This manuscript uses three logically distinct ``certificate families&#x27;&#x27;: stability (existence/robustness of fixed points of a completion rule), novelty (strict theory extension by adding non-definable predicat…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0003"><data key="label">The organizing picture: a three-certificate loop: The results of this paper can be read as a minimal calculus for building hierarchical structure while keeping directionality honest. The core observation is that three notions often conflated in informal discu…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0004"><data key="label">Stationarity is not needed for Sigma_T or data processing: The definition in Section makes sense for any initial distribution rho (it may be +∈fty if reverse-support is missing). The data processing inequality for KL is purely measure-theoretic and therefore…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0005"><data key="label">WLOG, we replace X by the image f(Z) so that f is surjective and every fiber B_x=f^ -1 (x) is nonempty. This does not change the induced sigma -algebra/partition or any pushforward laws. We will often refer to a lens f:Z→ X (equivalently, the induced finite p…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0006"><data key="label">Emergence and open-endedness (terminology): Throughout, emergence means the appearance of stable fixed points of a completion rule within a theory package, while open-endedness means strict theory extension (a change in definability or completion), not iterat…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0007"><data key="label">Finite theory package: Assume A_FIN. A finite theory package is a tuple T = (Z,\ f,\ Sigma_f,\ E,\ A) where: Z is a finite carrier set (microstate space). f:Z→ X is a lens (coarse description). The induced definability structure Sigma_f is the finite sigma -a…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0008"><data key="label">We use the symbol Sigma in two unrelated roles: Sigma_f denotes the definability sigma -algebra induced by a lens f , while Sigma_T(rho) denotes the finite-horizon path reversal asymmetry defined in Section . Context (and subscripts) distinguish these usages.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0009"><data key="label">Instantiations in this manuscript: The components of T appear throughout the paper in specialized forms. In Section , an order-closure operator c on a poset is an idempotent completion rule; fixed points of c are the ``objects&#x27;&#x27; at that rung of a closure ladd…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0010"><data key="label">Closure operator: Let (L, ) be a poset. A map c:L→ L is a closure operator if for all x,y∈ L : (Extensive) x c(x) . (Monotone) x y implies c(x) c(y) . (Idempotent) c(c(x)) = c(x) .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0011"><data key="label">Closed points / objects: Given a closure operator c:L→ L , its closed points (or objects of the theory determined by c ) are the fixed points Fix(c) := \ x∈ L : c(x)=x\ .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0012"><data key="label">Closed points are antitone in closure strength: If c d are closure operators on L , then Fix(d)⊆ Fix(c) . Equivalently: if d(x)=x then c(x)=x .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0013"><data key="label">One-step stabilization under iteration: Let c be a closure operator on L . Define the iterate c^ (n) by c^ (0) =id and c^ (n+1) = c c^ (n) . Then for all x∈ L and all integers n 1 , c^ (n) (x) = c(x).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0014"><data key="label">Closure saturates: Fix a closure operator c . For any x∈ L , the sequence x, c(x), c(c(x)), c(c(c(x))), stabilizes after one closure step: it is constant from the second term onward.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0015"><data key="label">Viewed abstractly, a closure operator acts like a completion rule. Iterating a fixed completion rule stabilizes. Thus strict ladder growth requires changing the completion rule (equivalently, passing to a stronger theory).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0016"><data key="label">Strictly stronger closure: For closure operators c,d on L , write c d (`` d is strictly stronger than c &#x27;&#x27;) if c d and there exists an x∈ L such that d(x)≠ c(x) .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0017"><data key="label">Closure ladder: A closure ladder is a sequence of closure operators (c_n)_ n∈ N on L such that c_n c_ n+1 for all n . The fixed-point sets form a nested chain Fix(c_0) Fix(c_1) Fix(c_2) by Lemma .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0018"><data key="label">The strictness relation is irreflexive and transitive on closure operators (by the pointwise order), and Lemma records the antitone behavior of fixed points. On a fixed finite lattice, any -chain is finite; the open-endedness modeled here comes from changing…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0019"><data key="label">Lean formalization note: The order-theoretic closure operator used here is formalized in the repository using mathlib&#x27;s ClosureOperator. The basic closure and ladder API lives in formal/ClosureLadder/Basic.lean . In particular, the iterate-stabilization state…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0020"><data key="label">Idempotent endomap: Let A be a set (or type). An idempotent endomap on A is a function e : A → A such that e e = e , i.e.\ e(e(a)) = e(a) for all a∈ A .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0021"><data key="label">Fixed points: For an endomap e : A → A define its fixed-point set Fix(e) := \ a∈ A : e(a)=a\ . We interpret Fix(e) as the collection of ``objects&#x27;&#x27; completed by e .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0022"><data key="label">Idempotents split: Let e : A → A be an idempotent endomap. Let i:Fix(e) A denote inclusion, and define r:A→ Fix(e) by r(a):=e(a) (viewed as an element of Fix(e) ). Then r i = id_ Fix(e) and i r = e . In particular, e restricts to the identity on Fix(e) and Fi…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P031-C0023"><data key="label">Order-closures as a special case: If L is a poset and c:L→ L is an order-closure (extensive, monotone, idempotent), then forgetting the order yields an idempotent endomap on the underlying set. The converse is false: an idempotent endomap need not be monotone…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
