    <node id="P032-C0023"><data key="label">Commutation of reversal and observation: For any law P∈Delta(X^ T+1 ) , f^ (T) _ # (rev_ # P ) = rev_ # (f^ (T) _ # P ). In particular, for the path law P_T^ mu,K from Section , P_T^ mu,K,f,rev = rev_ # P_T^ mu,K,f = f^ (T) _ # (P_T^ mu,K,rev ).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0024"><data key="label">Proof of the arrow DPI theorem: [Proof of Theorem ] Fix T 0 and abbreviate P := P_T^ mu,K ∈Delta(X^ T+1 ) and Q := P_T^ mu,K,rev = rev_ # P . Apply Lemma to Omega=X^ T+1 , Omega&#x27;=Y^ T+1 and g=f^ (T) : D_ KL (f^ (T) _ # P \| f^ (T) _ # Q ) D_ KL (P \| Q). By L…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0025"><data key="label">Detailed balance implies path-law reversal invariance: Assume pi∈Delta(X) is stationary for K and satisfies detailed balance: pi(x) K(x,x&#x27;) = pi(x&#x27;) K(x&#x27;,x) for all x,x&#x27;∈ X. Then for every T 0 , P_T^ pi,K = P_T^ pi,K,rev .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0026"><data key="label">What is ruled out, and what is not: Theorem is a statement about an arrow audit: it rules out certifying irreversibility of the observed process via D_ KL (P_T^ pi,K,f \| P_T^ pi,K,f,rev ) when the underlying system is reversible and started in the stationary…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0027"><data key="label">Antisymmetric edge labelling / 1 -form: An antisymmetric edge labelling on G is a function a: E→ R such that a(v,u)=-a(u,v) for all (u,v)∈ E .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0028"><data key="label">Exactness and potentials: An antisymmetric labelling a is exact if there exists a function phi:V→ R such that for every (u,v)∈ E , a(u,v)=phi(v)-phi(u). Any such phi is called a potential for a .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0029"><data key="label">Closed-walk sum / cycle affinity: Let w=(v_0,v_1, ,v_k) be a finite walk with v_k=v_0 and \ v_i,v_ i+1 \ ∈ E for all i . The closed-walk sum of a along w is S_a(w) = _ i=0 ^ k-1 a(v_i,v_ i+1 ). When a is a log-ratio 1 -form, S_a(w) is the (log) cycle affinity…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0030"><data key="label">Cycle rank: Let c(G) be the number of connected components of G . The cycle rank (first Betti number) of G is beta_1(G) = |E|-|V|+c(G). In particular, beta_1(G)=0 if and only if every component of G is a tree. These cycle-space facts are standard in graph the…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0031"><data key="label">Rooted parent map on a tree: Let G=(V,E) be a finite tree and fix a root r∈ V . For each vertex v≠ r there exists a unique neighbor par(v) of v lying on the unique simple path from r to v .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0032"><data key="label">Connection to log-ratio affinity: If K is a Markov kernel on a finite state space with bidirected support graph G , a common affinity 1 -form is the log-ratio a_K(u,v)= K(u,v) K(v,u) on each bidirected edge. This is antisymmetric on E . Theorems and show that…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0033"><data key="label">Thresholding and regularization: The theorems above are statements about the exact graph object (V,E) and the exact antisymmetric data a supported on E . Graph thresholding or regularization changes E and can change beta_1(G) ; such modifications lie outside…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0034"><data key="label">KL mixture decomposition: Let (w_i)_ i∈ I be nonnegative weights on a finite index set I , not all zero, and let (P_i)_ i∈ I be probability laws on a finite set Omega . Define W := _ i∈ I w_i, R := 1 W _ i∈ I w_i P_i. Then for every probability law Q on Omega…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0035"><data key="label">Best macro kernel: For each y∈ Y , define K^ _tau(y, ) := p_y^ (tau) ( )=Law(Y_ t+tau Y_t=y). Then _K L_tau(K) = _ x∈ X mu(x) D_ KL (p_x^ (tau) \| p_ Pi(x) ^ (tau) ), and the minimum is attained by K^ _tau .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0036"><data key="label">Conditional mutual information identity: In this finite deterministic setting, I(X_t;Y_ t+tau Y_t) = _ x∈ X mu(x) D_ KL (p_x^ (tau) \| p_ Pi(x) ^ (tau) ).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0037"><data key="label">Scope of exact closure: If every macro fiber is a singleton, then Pi loses no information and the closure deficit vanishes trivially. More generally, if all packaged future laws p_x^ (tau) agree inside each fiber Pi^ -1 (y) , then the minimizing macro kernel…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0038"><data key="label">Attribution is separate: The theorem isolates a finite obstruction: distinct packaged future laws inside a single macro fiber force a positive closure deficit. Which primitive or modeling decision produces such a pair is a separate question and is independent…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0039"><data key="label">Dobrushin contraction in total variation: For all mu,nu∈Delta(Y) , d_ TV (mu P,nu P) lambda(P) d_ TV (mu,nu).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P032-C0040"><data key="label">Strict contraction gives a unique fixed distribution: Assume lambda(P)&lt;1 . Then there exists a unique distribution pi∈Delta(Y) with pi P=pi . Moreover, for every mu∈Delta(Y) and every integer t 0 , d_ TV (mu P^t,pi) lambda(P)^t d_ TV (mu,pi).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
