    <node id="P034-C0027"><data key="label">One-step saturation: Let pkg=(X,op) be a closure package. Then for every x∈ X and every n∈ N , op^ [n+1] (x)=op(x). Equivalently, once the fixed package has acted once, further self-iteration does not move the result any farther.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0028"><data key="label">No persistent growth in a frozen package: Let pkg=(X,op) be a closure package and let x∈ X . Then x is not a persistent growth witness for pkg .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0029"><data key="label">Theorem is negative: a fixed package cannot itself sustain persistent growth. The bridge theorem turns that negative fact into a positive structural principle. If a genuine persistent growth witness is present, then one is no longer in the regime of a single…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0030"><data key="label">Strict growth requires package change: Suppose a persistent growth witness is claimed for what is being treated as a single fixed closure package. Then the fixed-package description fails. Equivalently, any genuine persistent strict-growth witness forces pack…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0031"><data key="label">Frozen slice: A frozen slice is a triple s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s), where Y_s: E→R, K_s: E→R, and C_s⊆ X. The functions Y_s and K_s are the yield and cost functionals of the slice, and C_s is its support cone.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0032"><data key="label">Frontier dominance: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, and let e,f∈ D . One says that e dominates f on s , written FrontierDominates_s(e,f), if Y_s(e) Y_s(f), K_s(e) K_s(f), and at least one of these inequalities is strict.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0033"><data key="label">Frontier efficiency: Let s be a frozen slice and let D⊆ E . An operator e∈ D is frontier-efficient on s , written FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e), if there is no g∈ D such that FrontierDominates_s(g,e) .</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0034"><data key="label">Agreement on the support cone: Let C⊆ X , and let e,f∈E . One says that e and f agree on the cone C , written AgreeOnCone_C(e,f), if e(x)=f(x) for all x∈ C.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0035"><data key="label">Slice invariance on the cone: A frozen slice s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) is invariant on its cone if for all e,f∈E , AgreeOnCone_ C_s (e,f) implies Y_s(e)=Y_s(f)\ and \ K_s(e)=K_s(f).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0036"><data key="label">Dominance is stable under cone-equivalent replacement: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , and let e,f,f&#x27;∈ D . Assume: FrontierDominates_s(e,f) , AgreeOnCone_ C_s (f,f&#x27;) , s is invariant on its cone. Then FrontierDominates_s(e,f&#x27;).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0037"><data key="label">Efficiency transfers to a cone-equivalent representative: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , and let e,e&#x27;∈ D . Assume: FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e) , AgreeOnCone_ C_s (e,e&#x27;) , s is invariant on its cone. Then FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e&#x27;).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0038"><data key="label">Theorem is the key comparative transfer result. It shows that efficiency belongs, on a frozen slice, not to an arbitrary syntactic representative but to the cone-equivalence class that the slice can actually see.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0039"><data key="label">Representative alignment target: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆E , let K⊆ D , and let e∈ D . One says that e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) if there exists c∈ K such that AgreeOnCone_ C_s (e,c).</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0040"><data key="label">Restricted alignment: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , let K⊆ D , and let e∈ D . Assume: FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e) , e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) , s is invariant on its cone. Then there exists c∈K such…</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
    <node id="P034-C0041"><data key="label">It does not claim that every efficient object is globally identical to a canonical representative, nor that every possible ledger collapses to the same family.</data><data key="ntype">CLAIM</data></node>
